import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout id="registerfile" title="Register File">

  <h1>Register File</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    Crusoe uses a much larger native register file than the x86 architecture
    exposes to software.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>64</strong> 32-bit general-purpose registers</li>
    <li><strong>32</strong> 80-bit floating-point registers</li>
    <li><strong>48</strong> shadowed 32-bit general-purpose registers</li>
    <li><strong>16</strong> shadowed 80-bit floating-point registers</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The large native register file is used for three main purposes:
  </p>


  <h2>x86 Architectural State</h2>

  <p>
    CMS maps the x86 architectural state onto native registers. The x86
    program still sees the normal x86 register model while the processor
    internally stores those values in its native register file.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, Transmeta's native conventions map registers such as 
    r0 and r1 to
    x86 state such as EAX and ECX.
  </p>


  <h2>CMS State</h2>

  <p>
    Some native registers are used by CMS itself. CMS needs registers for its
    internal runtime state as well as temporary values used while executing
    translated code.
  </p>

  <h2>Software Register Renaming</h2>

  <p>
    CMS can use the additional native registers to assign different values to
    different native registers during translation.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, two different values that would both be represented by EAX at different points in the x86 program can be kept in
    separate native registers.This gives CMS more
    freedom when scheduling native operations and by reducing
    dependencies.
  </p>

</Layout>